IMS provisioning: no dead end, and a timeout is not a refusal

Telus burns its three-attempt burst on most boots -- 168, 168, then 36 -- and
was then down until ModemManager restarted. It does not need fewer attempts; it
needs a fourth. The same request, byte for byte, was granted on a later attempt
at 48 s, 69 s and 464 s in other runs, each time with the P-CSCF pool that
registers.

**The dead end.** Exhausting the burst set IMS_STATE_FAILED -- a value nothing
ever compared against. The start gate only fires from IMS_STATE_STOPPED, so the
stack could never come back on its own. Recovery depended on an outside event
that happened to call mm_sit_ims_stop() again (a VoPS drop, a RAT change), and
on a network where nothing churns there is none. "Giving up until the next
registration cycle" was aspirational; nothing implemented it.

It now goes back to STOPPED, which mm_sit_ims_stop() already does, and the
enum member is gone.

The CP is still protected, by the guard that was actually measured to protect
it rather than by this one. The monotonic floor above doubles 30 s -> 300 s and
survives stop/start, so a burst can repeat every few minutes but cannot become
the unbounded loop that once put 26x Android's volume through the 4KiB oem
ring, delayed an IMS SMS by 11 s and 39 s, and hung the CP into its watchdog.
That loop had no floor; this one does.

**A transport error is not a decision.** schedule_retry() counted every failure
against the burst, including "Serial command timed out" -- which means the CP
never answered, not that it refused the subscription. On one boot a timeout hit
both stacks at once and ate the retry that usually succeeds. Only a failure
carrying a cause counts now; the cause is already parsed out two lines later
for the refusal signal.

Also here, unrelated and cosmetic: read_os_build() joins /etc/os-release's ID
and VERSION_ID with a hyphen, so DEVICE_INFO's build field and the UA's #BUILD#
are one token ("nura-edge") the way a vendor build id is. The spaced form was
briefly suspected of costing an IMS PDN and then exonerated -- the same build
registered both stacks on the next boot -- so this is tidiness, not a fix.

--- a/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-client.c
+++ b/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-client.c
@@ -4958,7 +4958,20 @@
         version = os_release_value (keyfile, "VERSION_ID");
     if (!name && !version)
         return NULL;
-    return g_strdup_printf ("%s %s", name ?: "linux", version ?: "unknown");
+    /*
+     * One token, hyphenated -- "nura-edge", not "nura edge".
+     *
+     * This string is DEVICE_INFO's build field and the User-Agent's #BUILD#.
+     * A vendor build id is always a single token (CP2A.260605.016), and the UA
+     * path enforces that itself by replacing spaces, so a space here only ever
+     * reached the CP through DEVICE_INFO.
+     *
+     * It is not a fix for anything: the spaced form was briefly suspected of
+     * costing an IMS PDN and then exonerated -- the same build registered both
+     * stacks on the next boot, so that run was variance. This is only a
+     * well-formed value.
+     */
+    return g_strdup_printf ("%s-%s", name ?: "linux", version ?: "unknown");
 }
 
